WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.607
Second-order curves have been studied since
ancient

2
00:00:03.607 --> 00:00:06.860
Greece, based on the paths of reflected light,

3
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:12.580
the structure of plants and other natural
phenomena.

4
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:16.193
If you take two intersecting lines and start

5
00:00:16.193 --> 00:00:19.560
rotating them around the angle they form,

6
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:26.161
then, where this meets another plane, various
geometric figures appear:

7
00:00:26.161 --> 00:00:31.460
an ellipse, a circle, a parabola, a hyperbola
and others.

8
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.688
It was believed that this produces a
cone-shaped

9
00:00:33.688 --> 00:00:35.870
surface whose cross-sections give these
curves.

10
00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:42.200
A long time passed before people began to use
this knowledge in practice.

11
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.460
These theories began to be applied in the
seventeenth century.

12
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:52.280
People studied astronomy intensively and found
that the planets move

13
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.730
along elliptical paths.

14
00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:58.221
First we will study the first curve of the
second order,

15
00:00:58.221 --> 00:01:01.650
the ellipse. We will look at the equation of
an ellipse

16
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:05.930
and work through various examples.

17
00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:15.050
Let us define an ellipse. An ellipse is the
locus of points in the plane

18
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:18.910
for which the sum of the distances to two
points called foci is always constant

19
00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:25.910
and equals 2a, where a

20
00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:30.660
is greater than zero. The canonical equation
of an ellipse is

21
00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:37.500
x²/a² + y²/b² = 1,

22
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.460
where a is the semi-major

23
00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:46.080
axis, b is the semi-minor axis, and c is half
the distance between the foci.

24
00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:53.530
The numbers a, b and c are related by a² − b²

25
00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.030
= c².

26
00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.767
The next feature of an ellipse is the
eccentricity.

27
00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:03.940
It describes how elongated the ellipse is and

28
00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:07.404
is defined as the ratio of the focal distance
c to

29
00:02:07.404 --> 00:02:10.660
the semi-major axis. It is denoted by the
Greek

30
00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.040
letter ε (epsilon).

31
00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:17.194
Let us look at the formulas in an example.

32
00:02:17.194 --> 00:02:20.880
We need to write the canonical equation of an
ellipse whose

33
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:27.690
semi-axes are five and four, and find the foci
and the eccentricity of the ellipse.

34
00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:34.190
We substitute into the formula of the
canonical equation. Now we find c. So

35
00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:39.910
the foci have the following coordinates. The
eccentricity equals three fifths.

36
00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:47.240
Now let us look at another curve of the second
order, the hyperbola.

37
00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:52.687
A hyperbola is the locus of points in the
plane for which the difference

38
00:02:52.687 --> 00:02:55.760
of the distances to two points called foci is
always constant

39
00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:02.060
and equals 2a.

40
00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:09.180
This difference is taken in absolute value.
The canonical equation of a hyperbola is

41
00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:16.140
x²/a² − y²/b² = 1,

42
00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.180
where a is the real semi-axis, b is the
imaginary semi-axis,

43
00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:26.800
and c is half the distance between the foci.
The numbers a, b and c

44
00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:33.260
are related by c² − a² = b².

45
00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:39.520
A hyperbola also has an eccentricity ε, as
well as

46
00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.550
asymptotes, lines through the center of the
hyperbola. The hyperbola

47
00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:49.970
approaches its asymptotes but never crosses
them.

48
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:58.770
If the real and imaginary semi-axes of a
hyperbola are equal, the hyperbola

49
00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:05.770
is called equilateral, or rectangular. Its
equation is x² minus

50
00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:09.390
y² equals a².

51
00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.596
Let us look at a hyperbola whose foci lie on
the

52
00:04:14.596 --> 00:04:17.970
Oy axis. Then the equation of the hyperbola
has

53
00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:21.379
this form. The eccentricity is computed by the

54
00:04:21.379 --> 00:04:24.640
formula, and the equations of the asymptotes

55
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.380
are these. Let us look at an example.

56
00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:35.980
Write the canonical equation of a hyperbola if
the distance between the foci is ten

57
00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:39.180
and the real axis is eight.

58
00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:45.740
If the real axis is eight, half of it is four.
If the distance

59
00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:49.545
between the foci is ten, then from the
coordinates

60
00:04:49.545 --> 00:04:52.360
of the foci the number c equals five.

61
00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.573
To write the equation of the hyperbola,

62
00:04:55.573 --> 00:04:59.860
we need to compute the square of the imaginary
semi-axis b.

63
00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:05.310
From the conditions of the problem we get the
canonical equation of the hyperbola.

64
00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:11.830
The next curve of the second order is the
parabola.

65
00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.484
A parabola is the locus of points each of
which

66
00:05:17.484 --> 00:05:20.740
is as far from a fixed point of the plane,

67
00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:26.460
called the focus, as from a given line,

68
00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:32.600
called the directrix. The canonical equation
of a parabola has this form,

69
00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:38.940
where the positive number p equals the
distance from the focus to the directrix.

70
00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:45.340
The focus of the parabola has coordinates p/2
and 0,

71
00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.750
and the point (0; 0) is the vertex of the
parabola.

72
00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.694
The equation of the directrix is this,

73
00:05:50.694 --> 00:05:54.490
and the focal radius is computed by this
formula.

74
00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:59.488
Unlike the other curves of the second order,

75
00:05:59.488 --> 00:06:03.280
depending on the focus and the axis of
symmetry, a parabola

76
00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.880
can be given by three more equations.

77
00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:12.990
If a parabola is given by x² = 2py, its focus

78
00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:17.850
lies on the Oy axis, and its branches point
upward.

79
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.560
This is the equation of the directrix of this
parabola.

80
00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:31.810
The equation y² = −2px defines a parabola with
branches pointing left,

81
00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:37.990
with its vertex at the origin, symmetric about
the Ox axis.

82
00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:45.190
The parabola x² = −2py has branches pointing
down

83
00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:49.670
and is symmetric about the Oy axis.

84
00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:56.540
So the equation x² = −2py defines a parabola
symmetric about the Oy axis

85
00:06:56.540 --> 00:07:01.840
with branches pointing down.

86
00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:10.890
Let us work through an example. Find the
coordinates of the focus of the parabola y²

87
00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:17.350
= 4x. The number p is the distance from the
focus of the parabola to its directrix.

88
00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:23.950
We find p: it equals two. From the equation we
see that the focus

89
00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:29.490
lies on the Ox axis and its coordinates are
one, zero.

90
00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.704
In a higher mathematics course,

91
00:07:33.704 --> 00:07:38.160
the study of second-order curves has not only
theoretical

92
00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:44.240
but also practical value. Second-order curves
are used in ballistics,

93
00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:48.060
astronomy and physics.
